Gather some information and start narrowing the possibilities.

Capture the exit value of the process that's presumably getting
signaled.  If the process isn't capturing the signal, or is using that
signal in setting its exit value, the exit value may help determine
what "signal" caused the process to be terminated.  If it appears to be
a signal that could be generated automagically by the system on
certain program errors (e.g. SIGSEGV), then it may be a but in the
program, rather than some other process signaling it (e.g. the kernel
is
zapping it when it tries to do something illegal).

If the timing is rather predictable, gather some information on
processes running around that time.  With suitable triggering on
capturing the exit value of the process, it should be possible to grab
a
snapshot of processes right after the process exits.  If you're running
process accounting, you should also be able to determine what processes
may have exited shortly before that (e.g. in case one of them signaled
it).

Look for likely suspect processes - if the process is getting signaled,
the signal would come from a process (possibly including itself) with
the same EUID, or EUID of 0.  Check the documentation for the details
on
RUID/EUID/SSUID and signaling capabilities if necessary.

If you have and enable audit capabilities you may be able to determine
precisely what signaled it and when.

If you have utilities truss, strace, tusc, or equivalent, you may be
able to run a relevant trace to determine exactly why the process
exited, and to potentially trace suspect processes that may be
signaling
it.


