Howdy Managers,

First, my thanks to Tom Smith from HP and Adam Price who responded with
very helpful information for my trashed two-node 5.1a cluster.

After a lot of wasted effort trying to recreate CDSLs on the
cluster_root partition, I gave up and installed fresh with 5.1B-3. All
of the important AdvFS domains were perfectly intact out on the SAN so
they were just reattached with advscan once I was back up and patched.
It worked out great because none of the Oracle configuration files were
on the member or clusterwide root partitions, but rather totally inside
the "data" AdvFS domains on the SAN. This was the
"swatting-flies-with-bricks" approach, of course. With time and the
proper backups, it was likely I could have recovered the cluster but I
had neither.

Perhaps just as an academic exercise, and maybe to aid someone Googling
for help on their own trashed cluster, here's what I figured out I
could have done. My apologies if this is obvious to every other TRU64
manager on the planet but it wasn't to me.

1). Long before any of this ever happened, I should have made backups
of each member's boot partition. Here's a convenient way to get one,
although you obviously need enough free space on the clusterwide /var
partition:

# vdump -0Df /var/cluster/members/member1/boot_part_vdump \
>/cluster/members/member1/boot_partition

You get something like this:
path     : /cluster/members/member1/boot_partition
dev/fset : root1_domain#root
type     : advfs
advfs id : 0x434c55ea.00004fe5.1
vdump: Date of last level 0 dump: the start of the epoch
vdump: Dumping directories
vdump: Dumping 70181654 bytes, 3 directories, 21 files
vdump: Dumping regular files

vdump: Status at Mon Oct 24 23:30:07 2005
vdump: Dumped  70181654 of 70181654 bytes; 100.0% completed
vdump: Dumped  3 of 3 directories; 100.0% completed
vdump: Dumped  21 of 21 files; 100.0% completed
vdump: Dump completed at Mon Oct 24 23:30:07 2005

Repeat as necessary, incrementing the member # to include all of your
nodes. Maybe do this weekly or monthly via cron depending upon how
often your member boot filesystems change (patches, new hardware,
whatever). These aren't very big and they take almost no time to do.

Now, make sure your clusterwide /var filesystem is being backed up to
some kind of removable media mountable by at least one member node,
since you could conceivably trash your cluster enough to not have
access to a network backup service like Netbackup.

Now let's skip ahead in time to some point when either through hardware
failure, corruption, or in my case, a clueless user who knew the root
password; one or more of your member boot disks are trashed.

***This whole procedure assumes your member boot disks and the
clusterwide root partition are on some kind of shared bus (in my case a
couple of HSG80s). If your member boot disk is on a private bus, you
won't be able to fix it from another bootable cluster member.***

2). Now, let's say the member boot disk for member1 is dsk1 and it's
trashed. Further, that you either already had a usable disk or added a
new disk to the cluster (hwmgr -scan scsi or whatever works for your
bus) and it's device name is dsk3. Because the original boot disk is
dsk1 and you want to now use dsk3 as the member boot disk after the
repair, you have to edit the member's /etc/sysconfigtab to use this new
disk. Sounds like a catch-22, right? well almost...

3). Since your member boot disks were all on a shared bus, you just log
on to any working cluster node and give this a whirl:

     a). clu_get_info -m 1
         This ensures your member is truly offline. You don't want to
trash a perfectly good node, do you? Change the "1" on the end or equal
whatever member # you're interested in fixing.

     b). /usr/sbin/clu_bdmgr -c dsk3 1
         This configures dsk3 as the boot disk for member 1. Write down
the output of this command, we'll need it later.

     c). mount root1_domain#root /mnt  (or wherever you like)
         Now the new member1 boot disk is read/writable by your working
cluster nodes.

NOW THE COOL PART:
     d). vrestore -xf /var/cluster/members/member1/boot_part_vdump -D
/mnt
         Boy, those backups sure came in handy didn't they? You just
restored the member's boot partition to the new member boot disk.

But we're not quite done yet. The backup contains a unusable
/etc/sysconfigtab. It's got inaccurate info for the swap partition, and
the major and minor numbers for the disk. SO...

     e). vi /mnt/etc/sysconfigtab
         Now, edit the swapdevice entry in the VM section to read
"dsk3b" and change the cluster_seqdisk_major and cluster_seqdisk_minor
entries to be whatever they were when you did step b.

     f). /usr/sbin/clu_bdmgr -h  dsk3 /mnt/etc/clu_bdmgr.conf
         This just restored the CNX partition to dsk3h. I think this
uses the CNX info from the cluster node you issued the command from.
These partitions are normally identical on all the nodes anyway.
Regardless, I can't think of a reason why it would be bad to restore
this partition in this manner. You might be able to backup and restore
good h partitions ahead of time with clu_bdmgr but I can't remember how
and I'm too lazy to look it up.

 You're done! Everything your member could do when you took the backup
way back in step 1, it can do now (please, no Beavis & Butthead "you
said 'member'" jokes). Of course you need to unmount /mnt and change
the bootdef_dev flag to use the new disk, then boot it into the
cluster.


I'd be pleased if anyone could critique this procedure or provide
corrections. As I haven't actually performed this on a real
multi-member cluster, YMMV.

Jack
