Perhaps. Do you have room in this private bus to install another drive?
Even better, do you still have the build disk that was used to
originally install TRU64 on the first cluster member? If you have the
former, you can pop a new disk in, boot the CDROM, install tru64 on
that disk and now you have a machine that can see your shared bus. With
the machine up and on the shared bus, you can use another working
cluster member to edit the trashed member's boot disk (restore it, make
it new again with clu_create from a good member, whatever) by mounting
it (you have to advscan if you're using advfs or monkey with /etc/fdmns
on the RUNNING cluster member to mount. Once mounted, you can use my
procedure from the first post to repair. If you have the build disk
still in the machine, you can just boot it manually from a halt
("chevron") prompt using that copy of the OS and make that bad member
disk available to the shared bus. Once it's there, you can use my
procedure I think.

I might be oversimplifying or assuming a familiarity with the cluster
that you don't yet have. Can you describe the cluster in a little more
detail?

Jack



